WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:08.360
The world's smallest
R environment. Running

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:11.900
R on a $15 computer.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:13.630
Hi my name is Mark.

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:16.580
I'm the founder of
the R4Pi project

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:20.040
and really excited to speak
to you about it today.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:24.680
I hope you're enjoying
the conference so far.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:28.640
The R4Pi project
exists to make it easier

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.780
to run R on the Raspberry
Pi family of computers.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.250
It's been possible
to run R on them

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.677
for a really, really long time.

00:00:36.677 --> 00:00:38.510
But the experience is
not necessarily always

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:39.180
a smooth one.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:44.120
So the R4Pi project really
aims to smooth that process out

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.180
to make it a lot easier
for folks to get started.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:50.330
We've got full documentation
over at R4Pi.org

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:53.930
and I'm going to show
you some more information

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:56.870
about the project today.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:00.310
So what is a Raspberry Pi?

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:03.040
The Raspberry Pi
computer is a family

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.940
of computers that was
first launched in 2012,

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:09.790
so about 10 years old now.

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:13.120
And they're created
by an organization

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.570
here in the UK called the
Raspberry Pi Foundation.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:22.660
The foundation's aim is really
to improve outcomes for folks

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:26.570
through computer science and
education and things like that.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.930
And the way that
they do that is they

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:32.800
create a very, very
low cost computer.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.410
We started out with a
computer that cost $35.

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.330
It's about the size
of a deck of cards.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:40.570
And then there have
been multiple iterations

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:42.260
of that over the years.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:46.030
And then at some point they
released a smaller version

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:49.240
of that computer,
which only cost $15.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.350
So now there are probably
three main members

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:56.170
of the Raspberry Pi family.

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.900
We'll take a look at those
in a little more detail.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.650
The Raspberry Pi 4
Model B is the most common

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:04.820
of the Raspberry Pi
family at the moment.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:10.740
It's the newest kind of standard
size version of the computer.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.170
This particular one is
in a nice rainbow case,

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:16.883
but the computer itself,
you can see how small it is.

00:02:16.883 --> 00:02:18.550
It's probably roughly
the size of a deck

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:21.160
of cards, something like that.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:23.990
And they start at $35.

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:25.600
That's for the one gigabyte model.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.200
They go all the way up
to 8 gigabytes of RAM.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.040
There's a four core CPU
at 1.5 gigahertz

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.430
It has an SD card slot
underneath for storage

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:40.730
and all the things you would
expect from a modern computer.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:47.060
So USB-3, wifi, bluetooth,
HDMI out, that kind of thing.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:51.840
Next, we have the
Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.510
This is the second iteration
of the Zero size computer.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:01.500
You can see that one is
just over an inch wide.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.280
A little larger than
a stick of gum, I guess

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.970
These are the $15 computers.

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:10.290
So there's a four core
CPU at one gigahertz,

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:14.850
512 meg of RAM, wifi,
bluetooth, HDMI out and again

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:17.915
the SD card for the
operating system.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:24.480
And then finally
we have the largest

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.370
of the Raspberry Pi computers.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.490
But that's only because it's
built inside this keyboard

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:30.100
here.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:34.110
So you can see the keyboard has
similar slots to the Raspberry

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:34.840
Pi 4.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:40.980
It's basically a Raspberry
Pi 4 inside a keyboard case.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.740
It's important to remember
that the Raspberry Pi is just

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.120
a computer.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.020
It's a small Linux
computer, and as such,

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:53.490
works very similarly to most
other computers, particularly

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.925
computers using the
Linux operating system.

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:01.050
So what about the
R4Pi project?

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:01.550
What is it?

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:02.080
What is it?

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.340
What does it do?

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:08.110
So, the R4Pi project
provides pre-built binaries

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:13.840
of the R language, first of
all, but also provides a CRAN

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:21.100
like repository where we make
available pre-compiled versions

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.610
of CRAN packages.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.290
Now we don't do all of CRAN.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:27.820
Maybe one day.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:30.110
Right now we're just
doing a subset of CRAN.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.560
So we're building
somewhere in the region

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.620
of 250, maybe 300
packages, something

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:36.650
like that at the moment.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:39.820
But we're adding
more all the time.

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:43.090
The way that this
benefits users is

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:48.040
that, if you're unaware,
if you're a Linux user

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.790
and you use CRAN, CRAN gives
you the source code rather

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:55.180
than a pre compiled binary like
they do for Windows and Mac.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:59.410
This is great because R
knows how to build them

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:02.110
and things like that, but it
can be very time consuming.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:05.080
There are often dependencies
which aren't necessarily met

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:06.920
in your build environment.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:11.530
And so the R4Pi project
builds those packages for you

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:13.930
and then makes them available.

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:16.840
In this regard, it's very
similar to RStudio's Package

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.850
Manager, but obviously
on a much smaller

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:22.810
scale. With
those packages available,

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:26.650
it becomes much easier for
R users on the Raspberry Pi

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:29.230
to install those packages.

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:31.010
Installation is much quicker.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:34.000
And there's no build
that needs to occur.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.550
And from there people can
get started much more quickly

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:39.220
with R itself without having
to worry about all of this

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:40.050
build stuff.

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:46.580
OK so let's take a look
at a couple of examples.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:51.440
In this first one, I'm going
to log in to the Raspberry

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.660
Pi via SSH from an iPad.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:58.070
So this is my little kind
of desktop iPad setup

00:05:58.070 --> 00:05:59.570
that I've got going on.

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:01.790
I'm going to use a
program called Blink

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:05.130
to SSH into the Raspberry Pi.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.580
This basically gets me a
kind of Linux terminal,

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:13.640
but on the iPad -- a butterfly
flew into my office and I

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:19.160
type the password wrong as a
result! -- OK, so we're logged in

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:21.650
and we're going to
run R, looks exactly

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:24.630
like R does everywhere else.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:28.350
What we can do, though, is
if we look at options("repos").

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:31.820
So if we look at the repository
that's set our default cran

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:34.430
is actually pkgs.r4pi.org,

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:37.820
which is the CRAN-like
repository

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:40.790
that we maintain as part
of the R4Pi project.

00:06:40.790 --> 00:06:47.990
So that repository contains
the prebuilt binary packages

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:49.530
that we've talked about.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:54.110
And when you install
them, it does

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:55.672
what R usually does,
which is ask you

00:06:55.672 --> 00:06:57.380
if you want to create
a personal library,

00:06:57.380 --> 00:06:59.600
if you haven't
already done so, then

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:05.160
it will download those packages
from R4Pi.org.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:08.852
Download will take a moment,
so we might just speed

00:07:08.852 --> 00:07:09.935
through that a little bit.

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:21.220
Then once the packages
are downloaded,

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:25.270
you'll see that it says
installing binary package curl

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:30.580
and installing binary
package Rcpp and so on.

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:33.580
This is different to what
would happen if you installed

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:34.760
those packages from CRAN.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:36.135
If you installed
them from CRAN,

00:07:36.135 --> 00:07:39.700
it would say installing source,
package, curl and so on,

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:43.210
and it would try and
compile those packages

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:47.440
on the Raspberry Pi, which
can be a very time consuming

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:48.350
process.

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:50.410
And obviously there
are occasionally

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:52.150
build dependencies
and things like that

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:57.730
which we've taken care of for
you with the R4Pi project.

00:07:57.730 --> 00:07:59.800
This makes installation
a lot faster,

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:02.650
and then once the
packages are installed,

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:06.370
you can load them as
you would normally.

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:10.580
In this example, you see
the Raspberry Pi desktop.

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:12.648
So this would be
particularly useful

00:08:12.648 --> 00:08:14.440
if you're using one of
the larger Raspberry

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:17.410
Pis, Raspberry Pi 4,
Raspberry Pi 400.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:20.800
And we're actually going to
do some work inside an IDE.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.470
So in this case, we're
using Visual Studio Code,

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:27.160
which is available in
the default Raspberry Pi

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:28.660
repositories.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:31.360
And once that's
fully loaded, you

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:34.659
will see that we have
the R extension installed

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:38.409
and that R extension
makes it possible

00:08:38.409 --> 00:08:41.650
for us to do things like
run individual chunks

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:44.530
in this R Markdown
document, for instance.

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:48.100
So in this case, it's just
running that chunk now

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:52.120
and you'll see that the
results come up in a window

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:56.170
on the side, in a pane,
on the side, I guess.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:58.670
And you can knit the whole
document from here as well.

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:01.180
So just hit the
knit button and it's

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:04.330
knitting the example
and then once

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:07.870
that's knitted we can go
and take a look at it

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:10.240
and it looks exactly as
you would expect it to.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.300
It's very kind of basic, R
Markdown document, obviously,

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:16.410
but hopefully
proves the point.

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:23.310
Then we can also flip over
to this slightly more complex

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:24.600
example.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.990
This is a really,
really nice ggplot2

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:31.620
plot example that I've
borrowed from the Palmer

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:35.160
Penguin's package website.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:38.490
And if we just step through
that with control and return,

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:40.860
as you would normally.

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:42.450
Step through.

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:45.090
And then when we go
through the last step,

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.810
it will print the plot in
the window on the side.

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:52.740
We're quite zoomed in here.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:56.400
So the for, kind of,
display purposes.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:58.550
But as you can see,
when we actually

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:00.620
look at the plot
in more detail, it

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:03.380
looks exactly as you
would expect it to look.

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.780
OK in this last
example, we're going

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:09.590
to take a look at a
Shiny application.

00:10:09.590 --> 00:10:13.700
So this is the Shiny
application, fairly

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:16.090
standard stuff.

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:17.710
And we're going to
run that directly

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:20.760
from the terminal
on the Raspberry Pi.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:24.880
So to do that, we
use Shiny's built

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:26.830
in function, which
is just runApp,

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:29.980
so Shiny double colon runApp.

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:32.290
And then when we
hit Return on that,

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:34.910
the application will start.

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:36.730
Now this is only
listening locally

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:38.600
at the moment on a random port.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:42.400
But if we go into the
Raspberry Pi's web browser,

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:48.350
we can take a look at what
that application looks like.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:52.570
So, we put in localhost,
the port number.

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:54.530
And there it is.

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:58.390
So it's a Bass diffusion
model visualization,

00:10:58.390 --> 00:11:00.620
the application's not
hugely important.

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:05.680
The point here is, as much as
anything, is that the R4Pi

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:08.230
project enables you to
do the things that you

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:12.340
can do everywhere else simply
and easily on the Raspberry Pi

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:13.300
as well.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:19.022
So it's a standard
kind of application.

00:11:19.022 --> 00:11:20.980
There are some inputs
which you can manipulate,

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:23.680
which change the
outputs a little bit,

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:29.050
and that works exactly
as you would expect.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:33.350
But what if you want to
run that persistently?

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:37.880
You want to run that say
on your home network.

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:41.000
Well, the Raspberry Pi can
help with that as well.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:44.780
Because of the Raspberry Pi's
low power draw requirements,

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:49.690
they make ideal servers
to run kind of 24/7.

00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:54.070
So the way that we would do that
for a Shiny application is we

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:57.630
would switch back
to the terminal.

00:11:57.630 --> 00:12:02.560
We would drop out of the
running Shiny application here.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:04.520
So we'll stop that.

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:08.030
And then we'll run it again,
but this time with a couple

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:09.120
of extra parameters.

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:12.930
So we're going to set
the host to 0.0.0.0.

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.080
That's basically
just a way of telling

00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:18.330
it to listen on the network
rather than just locally.

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:22.070
And then we'll set a port
so that it's easier for me

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:23.290
to find later on.

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:28.310
Once that's up and running.

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:29.588
We can test it.

00:12:29.588 --> 00:12:31.130
We can test and make
sure that that's

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:32.990
available on the network.

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:36.490
So to do that, I'm going
to flip over to my iPad.

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:43.000
And then once on the
iPad, I'm going to type

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:46.070
in the IP address
or the host name,

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:49.150
if I have one, for the machine
on the local network.

00:12:49.150 --> 00:12:52.570
I'm using the IP
address here and then

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:57.410
I'm going to put in the port
that we specified earlier on.

00:12:57.410 --> 00:13:00.020
And we'll see if that loads.

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:01.040
There we go.

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:05.240
It's the exact same Bass
diffusion model visualization

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:09.320
running in the exact
same way as before.

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:11.870
The rendering is probably
a little different

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:15.500
because we're using the
iPad web browser rather than

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:17.870
the one that's built
into the Raspberry Pi.

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:21.020
But other than that, it works
in exactly the same way,

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:23.720
the same inputs connected
to the same outputs that

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:26.935
change the plots in
exactly the same way.

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:36.480
One of the things that
I'm most excited about,

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:40.290
about running R on a
Raspberry Pi is because it's

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:42.600
a very small computer,

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:46.350
you can run them in places
where you wouldn't normally

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:50.790
run a 1000, 2000 dollar
computer. They

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:53.380
can be powered by batteries.

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:55.380
In a lot of cases,
especially the smaller ones,

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:58.830
you can run those from
a USB battery bank,

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:02.550
but you can imagine,

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:06.100
ordinarily with data
science, people give us data.

00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:08.220
So the data is
collected somewhere.

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:10.950
It's bundled together,
often not very well,

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:15.810
and then handed over to the
data scientist for analysis.

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:18.367
But what we can do
with when we have

00:14:18.367 --> 00:14:19.950
a small computer
like the Raspberry Pi

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:23.830
is we can put that computer
where the data is collected.

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:27.240
So that could be the data
collection device as well as

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:29.480
the place where the
analysis occurs.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:31.770
This kind of
concept in computing

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:33.780
is often referred to
as edge computing,

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:38.640
because you can take the
centralized resources that

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:41.100
are often used for
this sort of work

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:43.920
and you can move them to
the edge of the network.

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:46.260
This means that you
can run projects

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:49.260
in a lot more interesting
places than you might otherwise

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:52.320
be able to.

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:54.030
Because of the low
power requirements

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:55.060
of the Raspberry Pi.

00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:58.360
You can run them in a
car while you're out

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:00.540
parasending.

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:02.040
I can imagine
people running them

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:06.760
while they're on a building
site or in a digger.

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:13.120
Maybe even a farmer looking
after cows in a field.

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:15.800
In a forest, on a boat.

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:20.230
The possibilities really
are endless with such

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:23.770
a small, low power device.

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:25.870
There are many
different ways that you

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:28.390
could run the types
of projects that we've

00:15:28.390 --> 00:15:30.490
talked about here today.

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:32.290
You don't need a
Raspberry Pi computer

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:34.270
to do any of this stuff.

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:40.060
But for me, the kind
of low cost,

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:43.180
the kind of ease of
entry into the ecosystem

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:46.660
is a really big boon for
a certain class of users,

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:49.000
whether they're people
who are just coming

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:52.570
to R for the first time, just want
to learn, people who want to learn

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:57.430
Linux so that they can use R on
Linux because obviously that's

00:15:57.430 --> 00:16:00.430
more or less a de facto
operating system

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:03.640
for scientific and technical
computing at this point,

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:06.310
or for any other reason.

00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:10.510
Just having something that's
very low cost and very

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:17.080
accessible for folks is a
really nice way to help people

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:21.670
get into the R ecosystem.

00:16:21.670 --> 00:16:24.850
Thanks for watching
the talk to this point.

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:28.260
Please check out R4Pi.org website.

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:30.370
Check out the Raspberry Pi
family of computers,

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:31.787
if that's something
that you think

00:16:31.787 --> 00:16:33.190
you might be interested in.

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:37.330
My personal website
and Twitter details

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:40.270
are there as well
if you want them.

00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:44.230
Thanks again, and I hope
you have an excellent rest

00:16:44.230 --> 00:16:47.190
of the conference. Bye.

